   "id": "a1b2c3d4",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# The Ramsey–Cass–Koopmans Model: Numerical Solution\n",
    "\n",
    "## Overview\n",
    "\n",
    "The **Ramsey model** is a foundational framework in macroeconomics describing the optimal intertemporal consumption and saving decisions of a representative household in a neoclassical growth setting.\n",
    "\n",
    "### The Two Core Differential Equations\n",
    "\n",
    "The model is governed by a system of two ODEs in per-capita terms:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\\dot{k} = f(k) - c - (\\delta + n)k$$\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\\frac{\\dot{c}}{c} = \\sigma\\bigl[f'(k) - \\delta - \\rho\\bigr]$$\n",
    "\n",
    "| Variable | Meaning |\n",
    "|----------|--------|\n",
    "| $k$ | Capital per effective worker |\n",
    "| $c$ | Consumption per effective worker |\n",
    "| $f(k)$ | Production function (here: $f(k) = Ak$, linear) |\n",
    "| $\\delta$ | Depreciation rate |\n",
    "| $n$ | Population growth rate |\n",
    "| $\\rho$ | Household discount rate |\n",
    "| $\\sigma$ | Elasticity of intertemporal substitution (EIS) |\n",
    "\n",
    "### The Shooting Problem\n",
    "\n",
    "Given an initial capital stock $k_0$, we must find the **unique saddle-path** initial consumption $c_0$ such that the economy converges to the steady state $(k^*, c^*)$ rather than diverging. This is solved here via **bisection**.\n",
    "\n",
    "---"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "b2c3d4e5",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 1. Setup: Parameters & Steady State"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "c3d4e5f6",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "import matplotlib.gridspec as gridspec\n",
    "\n",
    "# ── Model Parameters ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n",
    "A     = 1.0    # Total factor productivity\n",
    "sigma = 0.5    # Elasticity of intertemporal substitution\n",
    "rho   = 0.05   # Household discount rate\n",
    "delta = 0.05   # Depreciation rate\n",
    "n     = 0.02   # Population growth rate\n",
    "\n",
    "# ── Numerical Parameters ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n",
    "dt = 0.1       # Time step\n",
    "N  = 1000      # Number of iterations\n",
    "\n",
    "# ── Steady State ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n",
    "# With f(k) = Ak, the steady state satisfies:\n",
    "#   f'(k*) = A = delta + rho  =>  k* is set exogenously here\n",
    "#   c* = f(k*) - (delta + n)*k*\n",
    "k_star = 1.0\n",
    "c_star = A * k_star - (delta + n) * k_star\n",
    "k0     = 0.5 * k_star   # Initial capital: below steady state\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f\"{'Parameter':>20}  {'Value':>10}\")\n",
    "print(\"-\" * 35)\n",
    "print(f\"{'A (TFP)':>20}  {A:>10.4f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"{'sigma (EIS)':>20}  {sigma:>10.4f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"{'rho (discount)':>20}  {rho:>10.4f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"{'delta (deprec.)':>20}  {delta:>10.4f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"{'n (pop. growth)':>20}  {n:>10.4f}\")\n",
    "print()\n",
    "print(f\"{'k* (steady state)':>20}  {k_star:>10.4f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"{'c* (steady state)':>20}  {c_star:>10.4f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"{'k0 (initial capital)':>20}  {k0:>10.4f}\")\n",
    "print()\n",
    "print(\"Note: k0 < k* means the economy starts capital-scarce,\")\n",
    "print(\"so it will accumulate capital and grow toward k*.\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "d4e5f6a7",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Comment on the Production Function\n",
    "\n",
    "This model uses a **linear AK production function** $f(k) = Ak$, which implies:\n",
    "\n",
    "- Constant marginal product of capital: $f'(k) = A$\n",
    "- No diminishing returns — growth does not automatically slow\n",
    "- The Euler equation for consumption simplifies to $\\dot{c}/c = \\sigma(A - \\delta - \\rho)$, a **constant growth rate**\n",
    "\n",
    "With $A = 1.0$, $\\delta = 0.05$, and $\\rho = 0.05$: $A - \\delta - \\rho = 0.9 > 0$, so the Keynes–Ramsey rule predicts **consumption always grows** along the optimal path.\n",
    "\n",
    "---"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e5f6a7b8",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 2. Euler Integration & Shooting Algorithm\n",
    "\n",
    "The system is solved using **forward Euler integration**:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$k_{t+1} = k_t + \\Delta t \\cdot \\bigl[f(k_t) - c_t - (\\delta+n)k_t\\bigr]$$\n",
    "\n",
    "$$c_{t+1} = c_t + \\Delta t \\cdot \\sigma\\bigl[A - \\delta - \\rho\\bigr]\\cdot c_t$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Because the steady state is a **saddle point**, only one value of $c_0$ (for given $k_0$) places the economy on the stable manifold. The **bisection method** identifies this value by exploiting the monotonicity: if $c_0$ is too high, the path overshoots; if too low, it undershoots."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "f6a7b8c9",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def simulate(c0, k0=k0, N=N, dt=dt):\n",
    "    \"\"\"Forward Euler integration of the Ramsey system.\"\"\"\n",
    "    k = np.zeros(N + 1)\n",
    "    c = np.zeros(N + 1)\n",
    "    k[0], c[0] = k0, c0\n",
    "\n",
    "    for t in range(N):\n",
    "        f_k       = A * k[t]\n",
    "        k[t + 1]  = k[t] + dt * (f_k - c[t] - (delta + n) * k[t])\n",
    "        c[t + 1]  = c[t] + dt * sigma * (A - delta - rho) * c[t]\n",
    "\n",
    "    return k, c\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "def bisection_shoot(k0, c_star, k_star, tol=0.01, max_iter=100):\n",
    "    \"\"\"\n",
    "    Find optimal c0 via bisection on the shooting error.\n",
    "    Error metric: symmetric relative distance from (c*, k*).\n",
    "    \"\"\"\n",
    "    c_lo, c_hi = 0.01, c_star * 0.95\n",
    "    c0_opt     = (c_lo + c_hi) / 2\n",
    "    history    = []\n",
    "\n",
    "    for i in range(max_iter):\n",
    "        c0_opt  = (c_lo + c_hi) / 2\n",
    "        k, c    = simulate(c0_opt)\n",
    "\n",
    "        error = np.sqrt(\n",
    "            2 * ((c[-1] - c_star) / (c[-1] + c_star))**2 +\n",
    "                ((k[-1] - k_star) / (k[-1] + k_star))**2\n",
    "        )\n",
    "        history.append({\"iter\": i + 1, \"c0\": c0_opt, \"error\": error})\n",
    "\n",
    "        if error < tol:\n",
    "            print(f\"Converged at iteration {i + 1}\")\n",
    "            break\n",
    "\n",
    "        if c[-1] > c_star or k[-1] > k_star:\n",
    "            c_hi = c0_opt\n",
    "        else:\n",
    "            c_lo = c0_opt\n",
    "\n",
    "    return c0_opt, history\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "# ── Run bisection ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n",
    "c0_opt, history = bisection_shoot(k0, c_star, k_star)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f\"\\nOptimal initial consumption  c0 = {c0_opt:.6f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"Steady-state consumption     c* = {c_star:.6f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"Ratio  c0/c*                    = {c0_opt/c_star:.4f}\")\n",
    "print()\n",
    "print(\"Bisection convergence summary:\")\n",
    "print(f\"  {'Iter':>4}  {'c0':>12}  {'Error':>12}\")\n",
    "print(\"  \" + \"-\" * 32)\n",
    "for row in history[::5]:   # print every 5th row\n",
    "    print(f\"  {row['iter']:>4}  {row['c0']:>12.6f}  {row['error']:>12.6f}\")\n",
    "if history:\n",
    "    print(f\"  {history[-1]['iter']:>4}  {history[-1]['c0']:>12.6f}  {history[-1]['error']:>12.6f}  ← final\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "a7b8c9d0",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "---\n",
    "## 3(a) — Optimal Initial Consumption\n",
    "\n",
    "The bisection algorithm identifies the **saddle-path value** of $c_0$. Starting below the steady-state capital $k_0 = 0.5 k^*$, the household must choose a consumption level that is:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Low enough** to allow capital accumulation (saving)\n",
    "- **High enough** not to over-accumulate and eventually violate the transversality condition\n",
    "\n",
    "The ratio $c_0/c^*$ reflects how much \"belt-tightening\" the household accepts today in exchange for higher consumption tomorrow. With a linear AK technology, the ratio is governed purely by preference parameters $\\sigma$ and $\\rho$.\n",
    "\n",
    "---"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "b8c9d0e1",
   "metadata": {},
