   "source": [
    "### Interpretation\n",
    "\n",
    "The three scenarios illustrate the **instability of non-saddle paths**:\n",
    "\n",
    "| Scenario | Initial $c_0$ | Capital dynamics | Consumption dynamics |\n",
    "|----------|--------------|-----------------|--------------------|\n",
    "| **Optimal** | Saddle-path value | Monotone $\\uparrow$ to $k^*$ | Smooth growth to $c^*$ |\n",
    "| **High $c_0$** (+20%) | Above saddle path | Capital **undershoots** / decumulates | Consumption higher early, diverges |\n",
    "| **Low $c_0$** (−20%) | Below saddle path | Capital **overshoots** / over-accumulates | Consumption lower early, diverges |\n",
    "\n",
    "**Economic intuition:**\n",
    "- A **too-high** $c_0$ means the household consumes too much initially, leaving insufficient saving to build up the capital stock. Capital decumulates, eventually violating feasibility.\n",
    "- A **too-low** $c_0$ means excessive saving. Capital over-accumulates beyond what is optimal, violating the transversality condition (the household is "leaving wealth on the table").\n",
    "\n",
    "Only the optimal $c_0$ threads the needle, placing the economy on the **stable saddle path**.\n",
    "\n",
    "---"
   ]
  },
