    "### Interpretation of Sensitivity Results\n",
    "\n",
    "**Effect of $\\sigma$ (EIS):**  \n",
    "A higher $\\sigma$ means households are **more willing to substitute consumption intertemporally**. When $\\sigma$ is large:\n",
    "- Consumption growth is faster (the Euler equation $\\dot{c}/c = \\sigma(A - \\delta - \\rho)$ is more sensitive to the interest rate)\n",
    "- Households accept lower $c_0$ to enjoy higher future consumption\n",
    "- Capital accumulates more slowly initially (more consumption is deferred)\n",
    "\n",
    "**Effect of $\\rho$ (discount rate):**  \n",
    "A higher $\\rho$ means households are **more impatient** and value present consumption more:\n",
    "- When $\\rho$ is large, $A - \\delta - \\rho$ is small (or negative), so consumption growth is slow\n",
    "- Impatient households consume more today, leaving less for investment\n",
    "- Capital accumulation is slower\n",
    "- For very high $\\rho > A - \\delta$, the model implies consumption *declines* over time (dissaving)\n",
    "\n",
    "---"
   ]
