    "## 6. Summary\n",
    "\n",
    "| Question | Key Result |\n",
    "|----------|------------|\n",
    "| **(a)** Optimal $c_0$ | Found via bisection on the shooting error; unique saddle-path value |\n",
    "| **(b)** Transition dynamics | $k(t)$ rises monotonically; $c(t)$ grows at constant rate $\\sigma(A-\\delta-\\rho)$ |\n",
    "| **(c)** Off-path sensitivity | High/low $c_0$ leads to divergent paths — saddle-path stability confirmed |\n",
    "| **Phase diagram** | Illustrates saddle structure; off-paths spiral away from $(k^*, c^*)$ |\n",
    "| **Sensitivity** | Higher $\\sigma$ ↑ capital accumulation; higher $\\rho$ ↓ capital accumulation |\n",
    "\n",
    "### Caveats & Extensions\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Linear AK production** is a special case that eliminates diminishing returns and generates **endogenous growth**. The standard Ramsey model uses $f(k) = k^\\alpha$ (Cobb–Douglas), which gives a proper interior steady state.\n",
    "2. **Forward Euler** is first-order accurate and can accumulate error. Higher-order methods (RK4) or implicit schemes would improve precision, especially for long horizons.\n",
    "3. The **bisection shooting method** is simple but may fail near explosive paths. More robust alternatives include backward shooting or collocation methods.\n",
    "4. **Stochastic extensions** (Stochastic Ramsey / RBC models) add productivity shocks and are solved via log-linearisation or value function iteration."
   ]
